    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d203">

                <pb facs="0322" n="210" sameAs="frus:frus1918/pg210"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 893.114/167</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">MacMurray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram—Extract]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Peking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-07-20T22:00:00+08:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">July 20, 1918, 10 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Your July 10, 4 p.m. I am requested by the Acting Minister for Foreign Affairs to
                    assure you that the decision of the Chinese Government to purchase the 1,600
                    chests of opium remaining in the hands of the combine contemplates its use
                    solely for medicinal purposes and that there will be no retrogression from the
                    policy of opium suppression hitherto followed by this Government. In promising
                    to convey this assurance I took occasion to express the regret which our
                    Government would feel if this were not the case. …</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">MacMurray</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
